※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ `⎕narrow(EXPR, ⌜TYPE⌝)` makes a value of a narrower type out of one of a
※ wider, and says so where it will not fit.  Nothing in this language widens or
※ narrows on its own, so a value that is to become one of another type is
※ written as becoming one -- and what makes narrowing different from widening
※ is that it can fail, so what it answers with is a result.
※
※ **What went wrong is worth more than the fact that something did.**  The
※ error carries a value of `⎕narrowing`, which is `overflow` above the top of
※ the type, `underflow` below the bottom, and `sign` for a negative number put
※ where the type has no negative values -- a case of being below the bottom
※ that the language can say more about, a reader told "sign" knowing which
※ mistake was made rather than only that one was.
※
※ The enumeration is the compiler's, because every program that narrows
※ anything needs the same three names: three spellings of one condition would
※ not carry from one file to the next, and a `match` over one would have to be
※ written again in each.
※
※ **Each condition is asked only where it can hold.**  A type whose every value
※ the other one has cannot be overflowed into, and a source with no negative
※ values cannot be negative, so those comparisons are settled while compiling
※ and none of them reaches the program -- which is what makes narrowing to a
※ wider type cost nothing at all.
※
※ A unit stays: narrowing a length gives a length, what changes being how much
※ room the number has and a unit saying nothing about that.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ it fits
    if (⎕narrow(given(42u32), ⌜u8⌝) ?? 0u8) ≠ 42u8:
        1u6
    ※ above the top of the type
    elif code(⎕narrow(given(300u32), ⌜u8⌝)) ≠ 0u8:
        2u6
    ※ below the bottom of it, where the type has a bottom below nought
    elif signed(⎕narrow(negative(⁻200i32), ⌜i8⌝)) ≠ 1u8:
        3u6
    ※ and negative where the type has no negative values at all
    elif code(⎕narrow(negative(⁻5i32), ⌜u8⌝)) ≠ 2u8:
        4u6
    ※ an unsigned number into a signed type of the same width, which overflows
    elif signed32(⎕narrow(given(3000000000u32), ⌜i32⌝)) ≠ 0u8:
        5u6
    ※ a width no machine has, whose ends are the type's and not a register's
    elif (⎕narrow(given(31u32), ⌜u5⌝) ?? 0u5) ≠ 31u5 ∨ five(⎕narrow(given(32u32), ⌜u5⌝)) ≠ 0u8:
        6u6
    ※ to a wider type, which cannot fail and is checked for nothing
    elif (⎕narrow(given(7u32), ⌜u64⌝) ?? 0u64) ≠ 7u64:
        7u6
    ※ the unit travels with the number
    elif ⎕drop(length() ?? 0) ≠ 9u8:
        8u6
    ※ and what it answers with is a result like any other, taken with `?`
    elif passed(42u32) ≠ 42u8:
        9u6
    elif passed(300u32) ≠ 0u8:
        10u6
    else:
        0u6

※ A value the compiler cannot see, so that the checks are made while the
※ program runs and not before it.
@[impure]
fn given(n: u32) → u32:
    n

@[impure]
fn negative(n: i32) → i32:
    n

@[impure]
fn length() → u8 ¤meter?⎕narrowing:
    let far: u32 ¤meter = 9
    ⎕narrow(far, ⌜u8⌝)

※ Which condition it was, written the way a value of any enumeration is.
fn code(of: u8?⎕narrowing) → u8:
    match of:
        u8:
            9u8
        ⊥(why):
            which(why)

fn signed(of: i8?⎕narrowing) → u8:
    match of:
        i8:
            9u8
        ⊥(why):
            which(why)

fn signed32(of: i32?⎕narrowing) → u8:
    match of:
        i32:
            9u8
        ⊥(why):
            which(why)

fn five(of: u5?⎕narrowing) → u8:
    match of:
        u5:
            9u8
        ⊥(why):
            which(why)

fn which(why: ⎕narrowing) → u8:
    if why = ⎕narrowing.overflow:
        0u8
    elif why = ⎕narrowing.underflow:
        1u8
    else:
        2u8

※ Propagated with `?`, which is what a result is for: where it did not fit this
※ leaves carrying the same reason it was given.
fn passed(n: u32) → u8:
    match narrowed(n):
        u8:
            42u8
        ⊥(why):
            which(why)

fn narrowed(n: u32) → u8?⎕narrowing:
    let small: u8 = ⎕narrow(n, ⌜u8⌝)?
    small
